## Criticisms (criticisms)
[024a4399f31e2549] (mini.criticism.v1)
All three proposals last cycle came back unrunnable: MINI_KERNEL_UNKNOWN, no kernel json.rfc8259.v1. That failure is mine. I named a kernel and defined it inline, but the executor runs only kernels in its registry, and no executor holds that name; a proposal whose kernel cannot be run records nothing, and none of the three predicted movements was ever tested. The execution record did yield one usable fact: the registry itself — mini.kernel.carries-because, mini.kernel.cut-count, mini.kernel.folded, mini.kernel.json-readable. The prototype's own check list was never supplied in this thread; that registry is the closest thing to it. So this cycle I re-anchor on it: one triple per known kernel, four triples, no two sharing a (kernel, transform) pair, and no kernel named that is outside the registry.

Triple 1 — mini.kernel.json-readable. The runnable successor to the unrunnable name: if the kernel does what its name says, accepting iff the input is readable as JSON, the trailing-comma boundary carries over unchanged.
Kernel — mini.kernel.json-readable.
Transform — json.trailing-comma: if the input's last non-whitespace character is the right square bracket, insert one comma immediately before it; otherwise return the input unchanged.
Input — [1,2], which becomes [1,2,].
Prediction: accept → reject. In RFC 8259 an array's commas separate values and may not trail the last value, so [1,2,] is not a JSON value and no conforming parser accepts it. Contingency: if the run accepts [1,2,], the kernel is looser than the standard its name invokes, and that looseness is then on record.

Triple 2 — mini.kernel.carries-because. Reading: the verdict turns on the input carrying a reason, minimally the word because.
Kernel — mini.kernel.carries-because.
Transform — text.drop-because: replace every occurrence of the substring because with a single space; if the input contains none, return it unchanged.
Input — it holds because the spec says so; the rewrite removes exactly its one reason-word.
Prediction: accept → reject. Contingent on the name's reading: if the verdict survives deletion of the only because, the kernel does not check what it is named for, and the run says so.

Triple 3 — mini.kernel.cut-count. Reading: the verdict turns on a count of cuts, divisions of the input; the most common textual division is a line break.
Kernel — mini.kernel.cut-count.
Transform — text.add-line-break: insert one U+000A between the input's first and second characters; if the input has fewer than two characters, append one U+000A at the end.
Input — ab, which becomes a, U+000A, b.
Prediction: reject → accept if acceptance requires at least one cut; accept → reject if it requires zero. Either way the verdict ought to move if cut means a line division; the direction observed settles the threshold, and no movement falsifies the reading.

Triple 4 — mini.kernel.folded. Reading: the verdict turns on the input being folded, i.e. containing a line break. This is the inverse probe of triple 3: same hypothesized structure, opposite direction.
Kernel — mini.kernel.folded.
Transform — text.unfold: replace every U+000A with a single space; if the input contains none, return it unchanged.
Input — a, U+000A, b, which becomes a b.
Prediction: accept → reject. Contingent on the reading: if the verdict survives unfolding, folded does not mean what the name suggests, and the run constrains what it does mean.

Reading the runs, for all four:
- Verdict moves and the pair is absent from the catalogue: a boundary nobody had recorded; the run writes it down.
- Verdict moves and the catalogue already lists the pair: confirmation, nothing new.
- The catalogue lists the pair as moving and the verdict does not move: a defect in the catalogue, or in the kernel's conformance to its name; either way the mismatch is on record.
- Triples 2–4 rest on the most natural reading of each kernel's name, since no definition of these kernels has reached this thread; a non-moving run falsifies that reading and is itself a recorded result.

Caveat: the catalogue block cited below — the record of which kernel-and-transform pairs anyone has written down, and whether the verdict is known to move under each — reached me truncated mid-sentence, so I could not check from here whether any of these four pairs is already written down; I propose them anyway and let the runs settle novelty.

Two further triples from last cycle remain convertible: json.single-quote-strings on the input made of U+0022, x, U+0022 (predicted accept → reject), and json.single-to-double-quotes on the input made of U+0027, x, U+0027 (predicted reject → accept), both against mini.kernel.json-readable in place of the unrunnable name. I hold them back this cycle so each check gets exactly one triple; I will re-submit them as standalone proposals next cycle or on request.

If the executor resolves transforms by registry as it resolves kernels, some or all four triples may come back unrunnable on the transform; that outcome is itself a fact worth having — it would mean transforms, like kernels, must be named from the registry, and I would re-submit against whatever transform names it holds.

